if(!mp[pair<int, int>(v.x , v.y)]) { mp[...]=true; ans++; }
This means they count unique cells visited by any particle at any time. But note: They mark the cell when they pop the node, not when they push. However, they also initially marked the start cell. In the BFS, they might visit the same cell multiple times with different states. The mp ensures each cell counted once.

In our Java version, we can do the same: when we pop a node, if visited[v.x][v.y] is false, set true and ans++. But careful: The start cell is already visited and ans=1. In the loop, we should check for each popped node. However, the C++ code does the check at the beginning of the loop for the popped node. That means even if a cell is visited multiple times via different states, it's only counted once. So we can do:

while (!q.isEmpty()) {
    Node v = q.poll();
    if (!visited[v.x][v.y]) {
        visited[v.x][v.y] = true;
        ans++;
    }
    ... rest ...
}

